A blank line belongs to the SHALLOWER of the two items it sits between, so
a loose sublist does not loosen the parent that holds it.

The items reach the writer as one flat array across every level, so asking
the item AFTER a gap whether a blank line belongs there puts one in front
of the FIRST child of a loose sublist. That blank does not fall between two
children: it falls between the parent's own text and its sublist, and
CommonMark then reads the PARENT list as loose. The next pass adds another
blank, and the one after that adds an indent level.

This shape needs a TIGHT parent holding a LOOSE child. When both lists are
loose the gap is wanted either way and the defect hides.

The `then-args` pass is the whole point of the case: the first pass alone
looked right, and it was the second that showed the drift.
